      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">01</span> The context</h2>
        <p>
          The Tools/Script Developers team does not develop the product. It maintains the scripts and the
          toolchain that generate the technical documentation for the software of the control units of the
          <a href="https://www.schaeffler.com/en/media/dates-events/kolloquium/digital-conference-book-2022/hybrid-powertrain/">DHT</a>,
          a transmission designed for hybrid vehicles.
        </p>
        <p>
          This documentation is produced with ETAS eHandbook: interactive documentation of a control unit's
          software, generated from its models and its code. Its content belongs to other teams (diagnostics,
          interfaces and others). Our job is the chain that builds it.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">02</span> The problem</h2>
        <p>Two steps of this chain were costly for the teams.</p>
        <h3>Unreadable logs</h3>
        <p>
          Each build produced a log of often more than 10,000 entries, most of them about diagnostics and
          interfaces. The teams that supply this data had to interpret it themselves, with a filterable HTML
          view as their only help. And errors only showed up at the end of the build: a wrong filter could
          empty a set of documents and make a build fail after several hours of computation.
        </p>
        <h3>A configuration copied by hand</h3>
        <p>
          For each delivery, the build parameters (document IDs, labels, filters, title page data) were
          entered on a Confluence page, checked by hand, then copied by hand into the configuration files.
          Only the import and the upload to Windchill were automated. The result: copy errors, JSON format
          errors, and no automatic check.
        </p>
        <p>
          My two goals: <b>validate the data upstream</b>, with quick feedback to the teams, and
          <b>automate the definition, validation and generation of the configurations</b>.
        </p>
      </section>

