        <p>
          I wrote a report generator in Python, started by Jenkins at the end of each eHandbook generation.
          It reads the CSV log, parses it with regular expressions and sorts it by category (diagnostics,
          interfaces). It then enriches each entry with data from Windchill (owner, state, function, change
          orders), through parallel calls.
        </p>
        <p>It produces one Excel workbook per category, stored next to the eHandbook files:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A data table: 20 fields for diagnostics, 12 for interfaces.</li>
          <li>
            Four automatically generated pivot tables, for example by owner or by function: each team sees at
            a glance the fixes that concern it.
          </li>
          <li>A log sheet, to follow the run.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Why Excel? Because the teams already used it: a report you can open without training is a report
          people read. I also added columns to the existing HTML view, in Jinja2.
        </p>
        <p>
          On the code side, an abstract class handles the Excel output, and each category is a subclass.
          Fields and pivot tables are described separately from the logic: adding a category means writing a
          subclass.
        </p>
