        <p>
          Second tool: a Python application, started from Jenkins. It reads three sources: the Confluence
          delivery page, where the teams enter their values; a Confluence settings page, which describes the
          mapping, format and validation rules, editable without touching the code; and the existing
          configuration files, stored in Windchill.
        </p>
        <p>Before generating anything, it checks each row in Windchill:</p>
        <ol>
          <li>The document exists.</li>
          <li>The label exists, with its date.</li>
          <li>The filter does return documents, and how many.</li>
          <li>The filter's parentheses are balanced.</li>
          <li>The software revision exists.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>It also flags empty fields or fields marked "TBD".</p>
        <p>
          The result of each check is written back directly into the Confluence table, with a status and a
          message: each team sees its error where it entered it. If there is an error, no file is generated,
          except in "force write" mode, meant for emergencies. Everything is logged, and the Jenkins job does
          not crash.
        </p>
        <p>
          If everything is fine, the tool generates the two configuration files of the build, a JSON file and
          a properties file. It also produces the internal and external variants of the filters, so that an
          external delivery carries no internal document. The files then go through a human review before the
          upload to Windchill.
        </p>
        <p>
          The code follows four layers: import, processing and validation, generation, integration. The
          trickiest part was extracting the Confluence tables reliably (merged cells, non-breaking spaces,
          encoding artifacts): I wrote a dedicated parser and normalization rules. The table format was
          designed with the project manager.
        </p>
