      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">05</span> The chain day to day</h2>
        <p>
          Alongside this, I handled about ten support requests on the build chain, tracked as change orders in
          Windchill, with review: installing tools on a Jenkins agent, migrating a script to the new internal
          Windchill access library, adding a cache, fixing an exception in the ETAS tool, a compiler fix on the
          build machines, a file naming convention.
        </p>
        <p>
          The environment runs on Windows: batch and PowerShell scripts, Excel automation through COM,
          dedicated Jenkins agents and several authentication modes to handle.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">06</span> Results and limits</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>By the end of the internship, the Excel reports were integrated into the Jenkins pipeline and delivered with every build.</li>
          <li>The configuration tool was available to authorized users, from Jenkins.</li>
          <li>Both tools were approved by the team lead.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>I have no measured gain to show: there was no before-and-after measurement.</p>
        <p>
          Still to do, and proposed as next steps: start the tool from a button in Confluence, without going
          through Jenkins; trigger the eHandbook build automatically when all checks pass; extend the reports to
          other categories.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2><span class="pin-name">07</span> What I take away</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <b>An international company.</b> Six months in an international automotive group, within an
            engineering team, with its review and change management processes.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>English every day.</b> It was the team's working language: I made a lot of progress, in writing
            and in speaking.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Autonomy and decision-making.</b> I designed both of my tools end to end, making the technical
            choices myself, then getting them approved.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>On the technical side:</b> object-oriented Python, regular expressions, Excel automation, REST
            APIs, parallel calls, Jenkins integration (Groovy, batch, PowerShell) and Windchill.
          </li>
        </ul>
